---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import DefinitionsNav from '../../components/DefinitionsNav.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import { cardSection } from '../../lib/docs';
import { PROVENANCE, PROVENANCE_ORDER } from '../../islands/labels';
import { fig } from '../../lib/data';

const notRecorded = cardSection(10, 3);
const classes = PROVENANCE_ORDER.map((p) => ({ key: p, ...PROVENANCE[p], n: fig(`rows.by_provenance_class.${p}`) }));
---

<Base
  title="A candidate, not a verdict"
  section="Definitions"
  description="What a row of the CEOL Universe says, what it does not, and why the universe records evidence instead of ruling on words."
>
  <div class="frame defs">
    <DefinitionsNav current="candidate" />

    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>A candidate, not a verdict</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every row of the universe is a candidate: a string, and a record of where it came from. No row says that a string
        is a word, that it is English, or that it is spelled right.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What a row says</h2>
      <p>
        A row records a string that passes the <a href="/definitions/grammar/">spelling grammar</a>, and the evidence for
        it: which of the <Figure id="projection.published_sets" /> published word lists list it, which way it came in, whether Google Books or wordfreq holds it,
        and, for a row of the English OpenList, what decided it there. That is all a row claims, and each claim can be
        checked against a pinned source.
      </p>

      <h2>Why no verdicts</h2>
      <p>
        Word lists disagree because they answer different questions. A spell-checker's dictionary, a tournament's word
        list, and a corpus's vocabulary each draw the line somewhere else, and none of them is wrong by its own rule. The
        universe keeps every candidate its sources gave and records each source's answer beside it, so a reader can apply
        a definition of their own and state exactly what it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is what <a href="/compose/">Compose a list</a> is for: a definition becomes a set of criteria, the criteria a
        count, and the count a list with the code that reproduces it.
      </p>

      <h2>What a row rests on</h2>
      <p>
        Every row falls in 1 of 4 provenance classes, by whether a published channel brought it in and whether a published
        list or a corpus holds it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <dl class="classes">
      {
        classes.map((c) => (
          <div class="cls">
            <dt>{c.label}</dt>
            <dd class="cls-n">
              <Figure id={c.n.id} />
            </dd>
            <dd class="cls-what">{c.what}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Even the strongest class is evidence, not a ruling: a row that every published list lists is still a candidate, and
        a row that only a corpus holds may still be one a reader wants.
      </p>

      <h2>"Valid," in the English OpenList's sense</h2>
      <p>
        The English OpenList calls the entries of its main list valid. The universe records that as a fact about the
        English OpenList at revision <code>08ae92b8</code>: its <code>eol</code> column is true on the{' '}
        <Figure id="rows.eol" /> rows that list held, and it means nothing more. Elsewhere on this site, a string of the
        universe is never called valid.
      </p>

      <h2>{notRecorded.title}</h2>
    </div>
    <section class="doc">
      <Fragment set:html={notRecorded.html} />
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .defs {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6);
  }
  .defs > :global(*) {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .page-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }
  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-5);
    max-width: 18ch;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
  }
  .prose > h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .classes {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 11rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-5);
    max-width: 60rem;
    padding-block: var(--space-4);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .cls {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    align-content: start;
  }
  .cls dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .cls dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .cls-n {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 650;
  }
  .cls-what {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
